& [JustificationIdentity] is the PreparedRuntime SJS rationale
  + [justification_uuid] is 1fdfb333-ea00-4f82-ac74-fa031fca636c
  + [conversation_uuid] is cab190f5-eda0-44e0-81a5-51f9605e0baa
  + [reserved_solution_uuid] is 05c9ee54-f70c-4a88-aa8a-0b31352ef0e6

& [Claim] is a bounded core PreparedRuntime experiment is the strongest next Cantor work
  + [support_type] is observation, deduction, constraint, and test
  + [support] is three Phase6 runs locate about 19.7 milliseconds of a roughly 22 millisecond 256-package request in repeatable admission and fabric construction while prepared query execution is about 45 microseconds
  + [support] is SemanticFabric already supports deterministic shared immutable reads
  + [support] is the user explicitly requested governed source, prototype, benchmark, evidence-based implementation, and complete governance
  + [confidence] is high for measuring a one-generation one-scope-projection design
  + [confidence] is provisional for production-corpus memory and service workload adequacy
  + [counterargument] is caching duplicates environment and fabric material, adds lifecycle state, and can become unsafe if scope or revocation boundaries are wrong
  + [answer] is retain only one exact-scope projection, share core gate and response construction, measure allocation-level memory, and reject activation if the complete evidence tradeoff is unfavorable
  + [invalidation_condition] is protocol-equivalence failure, cross-scope reuse, stale generation selection, incomplete invalidation, unbounded memory, absent heap evidence, adapter semantic drift, or insufficient measured benefit

& [SelectedApproach] is core-owned lazy exact-scope preparation
  + [why] is request bindings provide the independent environment and package authority bootstrap before cached semantic use
  + [why] is exact AuthorityScope equality prevents broad admission from leaking into a narrower request
  + [why] is one retained projection caps scope-cache growth while preserving the dominant repeated-scope workload
  + [why] is an atomic runtime slot separates immutable generation execution from activation and invalidation control
  + [why] is shared core helpers make equality structural rather than aspirational

& [Alternative] is one globally admitted fabric
  + [status] is rejected
  + [reason] is admit_package evaluates requested_scope and a globally broad fabric would erase a live trust gate

& [Alternative] is an unbounded map of prepared scopes
  + [status] is rejected_for_this_slice
  + [reason] is it increases retained and peak memory without an observed multi-scope workload

& [Alternative] is retain only a consumed environment-free prepared fabric
  + [status] is deferred
  + [reason] is it cannot preserve exact fallback for a different valid scope without a new supervisor routing contract

& [Alternative] is add live wall-clock expiry
  + [status] is outside
  + [reason] is current direct execution uses pinned environment time and unilateral cache-only wall-clock behavior would violate exact ProtocolResponse equivalence

& [Alternative] is implement caching in MCP
  + [status] is rejected
  + [reason] is semantic admission, scope, protocol, proof, and outcome policy belong to cantor_core

& [DecisionClassification] is reversible but safety-sensitive
  + [decision_type] is type2_reversible
  + [rollback] is retain execute_protocol_request, preserve request-scoped execution, and keep adapter activation conditional on the evidence gate
  + [premortem] is cache scope confusion, stale revocation, partial replacement, lock-held query execution, hidden heap growth, and selected-field equivalence could make a fast result untrustworthy
  + [mitigation] is exact structural equality, one projection, compare-and-replace, fail-closed invalidation, lock-release tests, allocation measurement, and full ProtocolResponse equality

& [QualityBar] is evidence before activation
  = must: satisfy PR-AC1 through PR-AC14
  = must: preserve all limitations and nonclaims
  = must: record any implementation contradiction as SJS rework rather than weakening the gate
  - never: treat a microbenchmark speedup alone as adoption authority
